      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)+10\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\sin \\phi}=\\frac{10\\cdot(\\sin^{2} \\phi+\\cos^{2} \\phi)}{\\sin \\phi}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\cdot(\\sin^{2} \\phi+\\cos^{2} \\phi)}{\\sin \\phi} & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{\\sin \\phi} \\\\ & =\\frac{10}{\\sin \\phi} \\\\ & =10\\cdot\\left(\\csc \\phi\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{10\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)+10\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\sin \\phi}=10\\cdot\\left(\\csc \\phi\\right).\n$$"
    },
